  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 329 AM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nToday and Tuesday...\n\nCold front will continue to exit the Commonwealth during the morning \nas cold advection continues to fill in behind the departing \nboundary. Clearing skies will be on going from the northwest to the \nsoutheast with most of the CWA being cleared out by Monday evening. \nA steady 10-15 mph wind will make temperatures in the low 50s feel \nlike it is in the low 40s for most of the day. The colder air will \nbe sticking around through the week with highs each day around 50. \n\nThe coldest night this week will be tonight thanks to clear skies \nand the ongoing CAA. Lows by Tuesday morning will bottom out into \nthe mid/upper 20s. As the next series of storm systems approach the \nOhio Valley by Wednesday and through the Thanksgiving holiday we \nwill have more clouds each night keeping lows mainly in the 30s. \n\nWednesday and Thanksgiving... \n\nA pair of closed upper lows, one associated with the southern branch \nof the jet stream the other with the northern branch will eventually \nphase together by Thanksgiving night somewhere over the Ohio Valley \nthen quickly lift and exit off to the northeast by Friday night. \nThis will result in a wet Thanksgiving holiday. \n\nThe trend in the models is that the upper low associated with the \nnorthern jet will be the strongest of the two as it works out of the \nUpper Midwest on Wednesday. As this upper low drops south, the \nweaker disturbance along the southern jet, will move along the Gulf \ncoast allowing both systems to draw Gulf moisture northward into the \narea. This will result in increased clouds and light rain slowly \ndeveloping from the south and moving northward through the CWA \nduring the day. \n\nThe deterministic models seem to agree that the two upper lows start \nto phase together during the day on Thanksgiving as a sfc low \ndevelops and deepens off the Carolina Coast. As the sfc low \ndeepens, a strong easterly low-level jet is expected to advect in \nAtlantic moisture westward over the Appalachians and into the Ohio \nValley. This will continue to keep steady showers around for most of \nthe Thanksgiving holiday. \n\nThursday night into Friday morning, both deterministic models lift \nthe coastal sfc low northward then back west along the OH/PA border \nas the upper low slowly moves eastward over the Ohio Valley. \nConfidence remains high that the primary precipitation type remains \nrain through this event despite a few ensemble members of both the \nGFS and the ECMWF continuing to show some light snow mixing in with \nthe rain. While that is not entirely out of the question, overall \nconfidence remains high that this is mainly an all rain event as \nthere continues to be fewer and fewer ensemble members showing snow \nwith each run and while a few flakes can't be ruled out the overall \nthermal profile is not supportive of snow. \n\nWhile confidence is high we will experience wet weather Wednesday \nthrough Friday morning, there remains temporal and spatial \ndifferences in the deterministic  that still need to be ironed out \nto give us a better idea on the overall impacts. As of now, rainfall \npotential across our CWA remains a beneficial half to one inch of \nrainfall by Friday morning. This remains unchanged from the previous \nforecast discussion. \n\nFriday and the Weekend... \n\nAs the system exits off to the east and northeast on Friday the \nupper pattern flattens out and becomes more zonal by the weekend. A \nfew weak disturbances are possible over this time but generally the \nforecast looks mainly dry with temperatures warming into the mid 50s \nfor the weekend.",
